---
/* ══ CARDS THAT INVERT ═════════════════════════════════════
   Three cards, each with the icon of the thing it measures.
   Point at one and it turns — ground, marks, icon, title,
   number and body together — while the other two stay.
   After Bencho's "Cards that invert", MIT, bencho.dev/licence.

   ── WHY THE ICONS ARE LUCIDE AND NOT DRAWN ──────────────
   The first version drew its own figures: a bootstrap cycle, a
   three-stage pipeline, a dependency tree, as inline SVG with
   letters in them. They were bad. Hand-drawn marks inherit
   every mistake — a wrong proportion, a label colliding with a
   line, a stroke weight that does not match the 1.5 the rest
   of the bar uses — and none of those show up until the
   drawing is on screen at every size in both themes.

   So the marks come from Lucide, which is the set Bencho's own
   components are drawn against, and which is 1855 glyphs that
   already share one grid, one stroke weight and one set of
   terminals. The three here are the actual subject and not a
   neighbour of it: `orbit` for a program that is written in
   itself, `square-terminal` for the thing it compiles to, and
   `package-minus` for a dependency count that is the claim.

   The weight matters and is set explicitly. Lucide draws 2 on
   a 24-unit box, which is 0.0833 of the glyph's own size, and
   the site's line work is 1.5 on 18 — also 0.0833. The two
   agree, so `absoluteStrokeWidth` is left off and the icon
   keeps its own proportion at whatever size the card sets.

   ── WHY THE GROUND IS NOT INVERTED ANYMORE ───────────────
   This section was a full-bleed band of ink, and that was a
   mistake. A page that is warm paper everywhere else arriving
   at a black rectangle is not a considered section, it is a
   hole, and no amount of restraint inside it fixes the edge.
   The reference gets away with a dark ground because its page
   IS dark. Borrowing the cards without the page bought the
   contrast and left the calm behind.

   So the band is gone and the section stands on the site's own
   ground. The inversion is now the card's own and stays inside
   the card: a surface at rest, ink on hover, with the marks,
   the icon and every piece of text moving together. A reader
   who never points at anything sees three quiet cards on
   paper, which is what the rest of the page looks like. */
import Orbit from "@lucide/astro/icons/orbit";
import SquareTerminal from "@lucide/astro/icons/square-terminal";
import PackageMinus from "@lucide/astro/icons/package-minus";

interface Decision {
  title: string;
  body: string;
  link: { label: string; href: string };
  icon: "selfhost" | "toc" | "deps";
}

const { decisions } = Astro.props as { decisions: Decision[] };

const ICONS = { selfhost: Orbit, toc: SquareTerminal, deps: PackageMinus } as const;
---

<div class="inv-grid">
  {
    decisions.map((item) => {
      const Icon = ICONS[item.icon];
      return (
        <article class="inv" tabindex="0">
          {/* No crop marks and no number, both of which this had.

              The marks were carrying the card's outline, since there is no
              border — which meant a decorative SVG was load-bearing for the
              shape of every card on the page, and the thing you look at first
              was a set of corner brackets. They are gone; the card is a
              surface, and a surface does not need to be drawn.

              The number was worse. 001, 002, 003 is a catalogue, and this is
              not a catalogue: it is three arguments, and numbering them makes
              them a list the reader can finish rather than three things worth
              weighing. It also had to be dim, which on a card that inverts is
              a second thing to keep in step with the first, and the first
              version of that pairing is what left the number invisible on
              hover in the dark scheme. */}
          <h3 class="inv-t">{item.title}</h3>

          {/* The icon is decorative: the title beside it already names the
              thing, so announcing it would say the same word twice. */}
          <Icon class="inv-fig" aria-hidden="true" />

          <p class="inv-b">{item.body}</p>
          <a class="inv-l" href={item.link.href}>
            {item.link.label}
          </a>
        </article>
      );
    })
  }
</div>
